There almost certainly isn't any "short figure".
That's trash trespassing from the icon of an
adjacent tile in the tile array as loaded into the
game.

I fixed that once, two or three years ago, doing a
complete preening of the official 32x32 tile set

    [in a couple of passes submitted one after
    another to the dev team, I missed noticing stuff
    one or more times (the best test is to become
    hallucinated on a level with lots of monsters
    wandering about, so that you quickly see all the
    tiles displayed ) before I got it all corrected]

but due to a miscommunication with a forgotten
member of the dev team

    [I didn't want my name, as mere preener of
    someone else's tile design, stealing credit by
    replacing the name of the much more
    credit-worthy original tile designer in the
    download page credits. That dev team member
    misunderstood my protest, withdrew my preened
    version of the tiles from the official downloads
    stuff, and put back the pied tile set while
    repairing the credit to the original designer.]

my fixes vanished into history.

The problem isn't with _a_ tile, such as the healer
tile, the problem is that the tiles are loaded into
the game as one big array,

    [and were apparently drawn by the original tile
    designer on that one big array rather than drawn
    individually and then assembled, presumably with
    a tool that didn't show array boundaries [as,
    say, The GIMP can], making it easy inadvertantly
    to paint an image across tile cell boundaries]

then subsetted or accessed at run time as separate
tiles. _Many_ icons of that original tile set
trespassed across tile cell boundaries, creating
strange little edge figures on adjacent tiles,
probably explaining the one you're seeing.

Sigh^2. Just because you work hard and have your
heart in the right place doesn't mean you can
accomplish anything useful in the face of personal
communications failures.

